/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(number totalMass, number unitsPerMeter)
\brief Generate a dynamic physical rig from the reference skeleton.

Creates bone-named Parts with shared PhysicsBodies, capsule Colliders and pivot
PhysicsConstraints. Bodies start dynamic; the Sprite follows the selected root's
translation. Its coordinating body stays kinematic and does not add simulated
mass. The initial body pose and velocity come from the current Sprite pose/motion.

Geometry uses the pose chosen by enableAnimationPose(). A branching bone spans
its farthest child connection points; one child gives the span from the bone's
own joint to that child. A leaf uses the longer axis of its image bounds' union
in bone-local coordinates, including reference transforms and pivots. Bounds use
authored SCML image dimensions. Missing artwork is diagnosed and skipped. Other
zero-length segments are skipped, except the selected root: it receives a 1 mm
physical segment converted with unitsPerMeter. The authored skeleton is unchanged.
Lengths are measured after reference bone scaling. Capsule radius is 10% of
that physical length. Generated Parts expose a rigid body frame in layer distance
units, so nonuniform bone display scales do not deform the capsules. Local/Sprite
transform queries convert that same rigid frame into the requested space. Included segment lengths determine mass
fractions; the coordinating body and unrelated Parts are excluded.

Each included child connects to its nearest included ancestor at the child's
current joint location. Distinct skeletal trees remain separate components.
Pivots have unlimited strength, no break threshold and free rotation. Components
share a nonzero collision group to suppress internal contacts; callers can edit
their filters. There are no automatically inferred angular limits or drives.

Repeated setup requires the original membership (all generated Parts, no added
accessories); edited membership must first be restored. It reapplies the original mass fractions,
scaling inertia proportionally while keeping bodies, colliders, joints, edited
geometry, velocity and independent loads. Changing unitsPerMeter or switching
from an explicit definition requires disableAnimationPhysics() first. Ordinary
physics.setMass() preserves the current, possibly tuned, mass fractions instead.
An existing bone-named Part must have the same binding/offset, no parent, unit
unreflected local scale, and no body or collider. Conflicts throw before replacing
caller-owned associations. Mapped offsets/bindings and Part removal
remain fixed until disabled. Change body control with setAnimationPhysicsMode(). Edit physical properties and geometry through the
Part's physics/collider objects; changing geometry does not recalculate inertia.

The Sprite's physics.getMass() reports component mass; physics.setMass() scales
all component masses and inertia atomically. Individual mass edits change the
total without redistributing. Whole-Sprite linear/angular impulses target the
selected root, including the world-point lever arm. Velocities, continuous
forces/torques and manual drives should be applied to the component bodies.
Single-body setupPhysicsBody(mass, inertia) and aggregate setMomentOfInertia()
reject while this rig is active. Inertia/angular-momentum queries on the Sprite
still describe its coordinating body, not a pose-dependent assembly sum.

\param totalMass Finite positive mass in application mass units.
\param unitsPerMeter Finite positive number of layer distance units per meter;
default 1 means meter coordinates for the fallback conversion. For example, 1000
makes the 1 mm fallback one layer unit. Other authored dimensions are already in
layer/rig units and are not converted by this argument.
\return This Sprite, for chaining.

\note Requires an enabled animation pose, Chipmunk support and a physics-enabled
SpriteLayer. Configure the layer before adding Sprites. Bone scales must be nonsingular; nonuniform authored bone scales and reflected
initial poses are supported. The Sprite root needs uniform scale. Set Sprite
scale/flip before setup: dynamic physical bones keep their initial effective
scales while enabled. Call
outside pose modifiers and locked solver callbacks. Initial Sprite/Layer snapshots preserve active rigs, edited components,
membership and control/recovery state. The example assumes a bone named forearm and successful pose setup.

\par Example
\code{.js}
sprite.setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(70);
sprite.findPart('forearm').physics.setMass(3); // changes the total
sprite.physics.setMass(80); // scales the current distribution
sprite.physics.applyImpulse(new pdg.Vector(20, 0));
\endcode

\see findPart(), setAnimationPhysicsRoot(), getAnimationPhysicsSetupWarnings(), disableAnimationPhysics()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::setAnimationPhysicsRoot(number bone)
\brief Select the bone receiving whole-Sprite impulses and root follow.

Selection normally prefers the exact name root, otherwise the bone with the most
skeletal descendants; ties use the lowest bone ID. This call takes precedence.
Before setup, selecting a zero-length bone gives it the 1 mm fallback on setup.
For a live generated rig, the bone must have an attached component; disable and
select the root before rebuilding if it was previously skipped. Live changes
preserve component identities and mass. Explicitly authored rigs keep the root
specified by their definition. Invalid selection throws without changing it.
Root overrides are runtime configuration and are not yet included in snapshots.

\param bone Bone selector in the current enabled animation rig.
\return This Sprite, for chaining.
\see clearAnimationPhysicsRoot(), getAnimationPhysicsRoot()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::getAnimationPhysicsRoot()
\brief Read the selected physical root bone ID.

\return Current rig bone ID, using automatic selection when no override exists.
Requires an enabled pose; does not require a physical rig.
\see setAnimationPhysicsRoot()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::clearAnimationPhysicsRoot()
\brief Restore automatic physical root selection.

Applies the same live-mapping validation as setAnimationPhysicsRoot().
\return This Sprite, for chaining.
\see setAnimationPhysicsRoot()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::getAnimationPhysicsSetupWarnings()
\brief Copy diagnostics from the last successful generated setup.

Reports reference-artwork inference, missing artwork, skipped segments and any
1 mm root fallback. Geometry remains inspectable through each Part's Collider.
Failed setup preserves the previous diagnostics. Rig replacement clears them.
\return Independent array of diagnostic strings; empty before generated setup.
\see setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::setAnimationPhysicsRoot(string bone)
\brief Select a physical root by its exact bone name.
\param bone Name in the current enabled animation rig.
\return This Sprite, for chaining.
\see setAnimationPhysicsRoot(number)
*/
